 "caption_en": "The first one took 11 minutes. Guess who.\n\nWho recognised Israel first? 🌍\n\n14 May 1948: Israel declares independence. 11 minutes later, the United States recognises it (de facto). 17 May 1948: the Soviet Union gives the first full legal (de jure) recognition.\n\nEnd of 1948: 17 countries. By 1950: 47. The 1960s: dozens of newly independent African states. 1979: Egypt, the first Arab country. The 1990s: 40 more as the Soviet Union breaks up, including China (1992) and Jordan (1994). 2020: the UAE, Bahrain, Morocco and Bhutan. Plus 21 countries with no date on record.\n\nToday: 169 of the other 192 United Nations (UN) members. 23 do not: Afghanistan, Algeria, Bangladesh, Brunei, Comoros, Djibouti, Indonesia, Iran, Iraq, Kuwait, Lebanon, Libya, Malaysia, North Korea, Oman, Pakistan, Qatar, Saudi Arabia, Somalia, Sudan, Syria, Tunisia, Yemen.\n\nRecognising is not agreeing: many of them criticise Israel too, and some have cut ties and restored them over the years.\n\nWhich one surprised you?\n\nSources: Wikipedia, International recognition of Israel (Feb 2026); US National Archives, \"U.S. Recognition of the State of Israel\". AI voice (Noam's own cloned voice).",
 "caption_he": "מי הכיר בישראל ראשונה? ארצות הברית, 11 דקות אחרי ההכרזה (הכרה בפועל), וברית המועצות שלושה ימים אחר כך (ההכרה המשפטית המלאה הראשונה). היום: 169 מתוך 192 חברות האו״ם האחרות.",
